WN.lb(1,{"mt_6lHBTwQPrS":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which question word asks about a place?","choices":["Where","When","Why"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where and when sound alike. Which one is about a place?","explain":"Where asks about a place: Where is the dog? Under the tree."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"The answer is \"By bus.\" Which word started the question?","choices":["How","Who","When"],"answer":0,"hint":"By bus tells you the way something happens.","explain":"By bus is the way, so the question was How: How did you get to school?"},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"The answer is \"Grandma.\" Which question fits?","choices":["Who is coming to tea?","What is for tea?","When is tea?"],"answer":0,"hint":"Grandma is a person. Which word asks about a person?","explain":"Grandma is a person, so the question starts with Who."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which sentence is a question?","choices":["Who ate the cake?","When it rains, I wear boots.","Tom ate the cake."],"answer":0,"hint":"A question asks something. Starting with when is not enough.","explain":"Who ate the cake? asks something. When it rains, I wear boots starts with when but tells you something."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Max has {n} stickers. Mum asks, \"What is on your stickers?\" Which answer fits?","choices":["Dinosaurs.","In my bag.","Yesterday."],"answer":0,"hint":"What asks about a thing. Which answer is a thing?","explain":"What wants a thing, so dinosaurs fits. In my bag answers Where and yesterday answers When.","params":{"n":[4,20]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Kim is {n}. She wants to know how old Jo is and writes \"How old are you\". What goes at the end?","choices":["a question mark","a full stop","nothing"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does it ask, or does it tell?","explain":"Kim is {n} and is asking Jo something, so \"How old are you?\" needs a question mark.","params":{"n":[5,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"\"When is your party?\" Which is the best answer?","choices":["On Saturday.","At my house.","Because I am six."],"answer":0,"hint":"When wants a time. Which answer is a time?","explain":"When asks about a time, so on Saturday fits. At my house answers Where; because I am six answers Why."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which word often starts the answer to a why question?","choices":["because","under","at"],"answer":0,"hint":"Why asks for a reason. Which word gives a reason?","explain":"A why question wants a reason, and reasons often start with because."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which question word asks about a person?","choices":["Who","Where","When"],"answer":0,"hint":"In the park picture, which word lit up Mia and Sam?","explain":"Who asks about a person: Who is kicking the ball? Mia is."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"The answer is \"At {n} o'clock.\" Which word started the question?","choices":["When","Where","Who"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is {n} o'clock a person, a place or a time?","explain":"{n} o'clock is a time, so the question started with When.","params":{"n":[1,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"\"Why is Leo crying?\" Which is the best answer?","choices":["Because he fell over.","In the playground.","At playtime."],"answer":0,"hint":"Why wants a reason. Which answer starts with because?","explain":"Why asks for a reason: because he fell over. In the playground answers Where, and at playtime answers When."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Ella has {n} toy cars. She asks Dad about the red one. Which question ends with a question mark?","choices":["Where is my red car?","My red car is under the bed","I like my red car"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one asks something and wants an answer?","explain":"Where is my red car? asks something, so it ends with a question mark. The other two tell, so they would end with a full stop.","params":{"n":[3,9]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Some words start a question. Who, what, where, when, why and how.","Each word wants a different answer. Who wants a person. What wants a thing. Where wants a place.","When wants a time. Why wants a reason. You often say because. How wants the way. By bus! On my bike!","A question asks. It ends with a question mark. It looks like a hook: ?"],"adult":["The six wh-question words (who, what, where, when, why, how) each signal the type of information wanted: a person, a thing or action, a place, a time, a reason or a manner. Children who can match the word to the kind of answer understand stories and instructions better and can ask for exactly what they want to know. The US Common Core standard L.K.1d asks five-year-olds to understand and use these words, and the English national curriculum adds the question mark in Year 1.","Who, what and where are usually understood first, and when, why and how later, because they deal with time, cause and manner, which are less concrete than people and places. Keep it concrete: ask about a picture, a book or what is happening now.","Common mistakes: mixing up where and when, which sound alike, so a child answers a when question with a place; answering a why question with what happened instead of a reason; thinking any sentence that starts with a question word is a question, as in When it rains, I wear boots; and leaving off the question mark or using a full stop.","At home, play a picture game. You ask one question with each word about a page of a book, and your child points or answers. Then swap: your child asks you, and you check together which word fits the answer you gave. Listen for because after why."]},"familyTip":"Pick a picture in a book and take turns asking one who, what, where, when, why and how question about it. If an answer doesn't fit, ask \"Does that answer the word we used?\"","keyIdea":"Question words tell you what kind of answer is wanted: who asks about a person, what about a thing, where about a place, when about a time, why for a reason (often with because) and how about the way something happens. A question ends with a question mark.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does this answer tell you a place or a time? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["A place: answers Where","A time: answers When"],"items":[["In the garden",0],["Under the bed",0],["At the park",0],["At bedtime",1],["On Monday",1],["After lunch",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagqw","caption":"Flagship lab: see what each question word asks about, answer questions about a park picture, pick the word for an answer, and end sentences with ? or .","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_9yFAtUkoYr":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which sentence is clear?","choices":["Dan dropped his cup, and the cup broke.","Dan put the cup on the plate, and it broke.","He dropped it, and it broke."],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for a pronoun that could mean two things, or one with no name to point to.","explain":"Saying the cup again makes it clear. In the second, it could be the cup or the plate. In the third, we never learn who he is."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"My {n} friends and I made the cake ___.","choices":["ourselves","ourself","us"],"answer":0,"hint":"My friends and I means we. What is the -self word for we?","explain":"We did it, so use ourselves. That is true for all {n} friends and you together.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"\"The cat chased the {n} mice until ___ hid.\" Which is clearest?","choices":["the mice","it","he"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who hid? A pronoun could point to the cat or the mice.","explain":"Saying the mice makes it clear that all {n} mice hid. It or he could be the cat.","params":{"n":[2,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which pronoun fits? \"The tree lost ___ leaves.\"","choices":["its","their","her"],"answer":0,"hint":"A tree is one thing, not a person.","explain":"One thing that is not a person takes its: the tree lost its leaves. No apostrophe here."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A child changes every Sam in a story to he, even the first one. What goes wrong?","choices":["The reader never finds out who he is","Nothing, it is shorter","The story gets too long"],"answer":0,"hint":"A pronoun has to point back to a name.","explain":"A pronoun points back to a name. If Sam is never named, the reader cannot know who he is."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"\"Nia saw ___ in the mirror.\" Nia is looking at her own face. Which word?","choices":["herself","her","sheself"],"answer":0,"hint":"Nia sees her own self. Her alone would mean another girl.","explain":"Herself loops back to Nia. Her would mean she saw some other girl, and sheself is not a word."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"\"Mum told Gran she was tired.\" It was Mum who was tired. Which is the clearest fix?","choices":["Mum said to Gran, \"I am tired.\"","She told her she was tired.","Mum told Gran she was very tired."],"answer":0,"hint":"Make it plain who was tired. Could anyone still mix them up?","explain":"\"I am tired\" can only mean Mum, the speaker. More pronouns, or adding very, leave it muddled."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which is the best way to write \"Kim rode Kim's bike\"?","choices":["Kim rode her bike.","She rode her bike.","Kim rode Kim's bike."],"answer":0,"hint":"Keep the name once, then use a pronoun for the repeat.","explain":"Kim rode her bike names Kim once and then her points back. She rode her bike never says who she is."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"The {n} ducks shook ___ wings. Which word fits?","choices":["their","its","his"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is it one duck or many ducks?","explain":"There are {n} ducks, more than one, so the pronoun is their: they shook their wings.","params":{"n":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"\"Lucy told Meg she had won.\" What is the problem?","choices":["She could be Lucy or Meg","There is no problem","It should say he"],"answer":0,"hint":"Ask: who had won? Could it be two people?","explain":"She could point to Lucy or to Meg, so we cannot tell who won. A name would fix it."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Jay painted all {n} pictures ___.","choices":["himself","hisself","him"],"answer":0,"hint":"Jay did it to or for his own self. Which -self word is standard?","explain":"Jay painted all {n} pictures himself. Hisself is not standard, and him would mean someone else.","params":{"n":[3,10]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Sam packed Sam's bag. That sounds odd! We swap the second Sam for a short word: Sam packed his bag. Words like he, his, she, her, it and they are pronouns.","Say the name first. Then the pronoun points back to it. The pronoun must fit. Mia lost her hat. The dogs wagged their tails. The box fell, and it broke.","Watch out for muddles. Tom told Jack he was late. Who was late? We can't tell! Use a name to fix it: Tom told Jack, \"I am late.\"","When you do something to you, add self. I made it myself. Ben hurt himself. They helped themselves. Hisself and theirselves are not right."],"adult":["At seven to nine, children learn to use pronouns to avoid repetition and to link sentences together. They also learn to spot when a pronoun is unclear. This is part of the Year 3 and 4 grammar programme. It covers personal pronouns (he, she, it, they), possessives (his, her, their, our) and reflexive pronouns (myself, himself, themselves).","The lab gives each skill its own action. Children tap repeated names to swap them, but must keep the first mention. They fill gaps while an arrow points back to the noun. They pick a clear rewrite when he could mean two people. They choose -self words that loop back to the doer. Then they find the muddled pronoun in short passages.","Common mistakes: replacing every name, including the first, so the reader never learns who he is; mismatching number, as in the dogs wagged its tails; leaving two possible people for one pronoun; and the non-standard forms hisself and theirselves. Another slip is using him or her where a -self word is needed, which changes the meaning.","To help, read your child's writing aloud and pause at each he, she or it. Ask: who is that? If there are two answers, add the name."]},"familyTip":"When your child tells a story about two friends, stop at a he or she and ask, \"Which one?\" Then help them put the name back in so it is clear.","keyIdea":"Pronouns such as he, she, it and they stand in for a noun so you do not repeat it. Say the name first, then the pronoun points back to it, and it must match: one or many, a person or a thing. If a pronoun could mean two people, use a name or reword it. When someone does something to their own self, use a -self word such as myself or themselves.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is each sentence clear, or could the pronoun mean two things? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["✅ Clear","❓ Muddled"],"items":[["Nell fed her cat.",0],["The birds built their nest.",0],["I washed myself.",0],["The cup fell, and it broke.",0],["Ali saw Ben, and he ran.",1],["Mum told Gran she was tired.",1],["The boys met the men, and they won.",1],["Amy and Zoe left. She cried.",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagpron","caption":"Flagship lab: swap repeated names for pronouns, match pronouns to nouns, fix sentences where he could mean two people, and pick -self words.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_yBJyCfhtem":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which word is a verb?","choices":["eat","cake","kitchen"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one sounds right after I can?","explain":"Eat is a verb: I can eat. Cake names a thing and kitchen names a place, so they are nouns."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which one is a sentence?","choices":["The bird sings.","The sings bird.","Sings the bird."],"answer":0,"hint":"First say who it is, then what it does.","explain":"The bird sings. The naming word bird comes first, then the doing word sings."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Is sleep a verb?","choices":["Yes, it is something you do","No, you keep still","No, it names a thing"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does I can sleep sound right?","explain":"Sleep is a verb. It is something you do, even though you keep still."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which word names a person?","choices":["teacher","run","beach"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for a word for someone, not a place or an action.","explain":"Teacher names a person, so it is a noun. Beach is a noun for a place, and run is a verb."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Mia reads {n} books. Which word is the noun that names a thing?","choices":["books","reads","Mia"],"answer":0,"hint":"Mia is a person. Which word names a thing?","explain":"Books names a thing, so it is a noun. Mia read {n} of them, and reads is the verb.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"The cat sits on the mat. Is the word the a noun?","choices":["No, it is a helper word","Yes, it names a thing","Yes, it is a doing word"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does the name anything by itself?","explain":"The is a helper word. It points to a noun, like the cat or the mat, but it is not a noun."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"{n} ducks swim in the pond. Which word is the verb?","choices":["swim","ducks","pond"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for the doing word. What do the ducks do?","explain":"Swim is the verb. It tells what the {n} ducks do. Ducks and pond are nouns.","params":{"n":[2,7]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which word is a noun?","choices":["table","hop","sing"],"answer":0,"hint":"Try the test: which one sounds right after the?","explain":"Table is a noun. It names a thing, and the table sounds right. Hop and sing are doing words."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Dad and Ali walk to the park {n} times a week. Which word is a verb?","choices":["walk","park","Dad"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which word tells what they do?","explain":"Walk is the verb. It tells what Dad and Ali do {n} times a week. Park and Dad are nouns.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Is the word school a noun?","choices":["Yes, it names a place","No, you cannot hold it","No, it is a doing word"],"answer":0,"hint":"A noun can name a person, a place or a thing.","explain":"School names a place, so it is a noun. A noun does not have to be something you can hold."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"The frog jumps {n} times. Which word tells what the frog does?","choices":["jumps","frog","The"],"answer":0,"hint":"Ask: what does the frog do?","explain":"Jumps is the verb: the frog jumps {n} times. Frog is the noun that says who.","params":{"n":[2,8]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A noun is a naming word. Girl, dog, ball and tree are nouns. A noun can name a person, a place or a thing. Mum is a person. A park is a place. A cup is a thing.","A verb is a doing word. Run, jump, eat and sing are verbs. Sleep is a verb too. You do it, even when you lie still!","Not sure? Try two tests. Say the word after the. The cake sounds right, so cake is a noun. Now say I can and the word. I can swim sounds right, so swim is a verb.","Put them together to make a sentence. Who is it? The dog. What does it do? It barks. The dog barks. Start with a capital letter. End with a full stop."],"adult":["In the first year of school, children learn that words do different jobs. Nouns name people, places and things (teacher, school, bike), and verbs name actions or states (run, eat, sleep). Using these words correctly in speech comes first; spotting them in a short written sentence comes next. The US Common Core asks for this in kindergarten, and the English national curriculum teaches the terms noun and verb by Year 2.","Two quick tests help a five or six year old sort a word. If it fits after the or a, it is probably a noun: the cake, a bed. If it fits after I can or they, it is probably a verb: I can read, they sing. Children then build the simplest sentence pattern, a noun phrase followed by a verb, such as The cat sleeps.","Common mistakes to watch for: thinking a noun must be something you can hold, so park or school are left out; thinking a verb must involve movement, so sleep or think are left out; calling the a noun; and putting the verb first, as in The sleeps cat. Some words can be both, like a drink and I drink, so judge the word by its job in the sentence.","In the lab your child taps the nouns in a park picture and meets two doing-word tags, sorts nouns into person, place and thing, drags verbs onto Sam to make him run, jump, eat and sleep while a noun card does nothing, sorts words with the two tests, and builds three sentences that match a picture."]},"familyTip":"Play Doing or Naming at teatime: say a word such as spoon, stir or kitchen, and your child shouts naming or doing, then acts out the doing words. Point at something and ask, \"What is it? What does it do?\" to make a two-word sentence together, like the kettle boils.","keyIdea":"A noun is a naming word: it names a person, a place or a thing, like girl, park or cup. A verb is a doing word: it tells what someone or something does, like run, eat or sleep. A simple sentence often has a noun that says who, then a verb that says what they do: The dog barks.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Naming word or doing word? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["🏷 Noun (naming)","🏃 Verb (doing)"],"items":[["apple",0],["teacher",0],["garden",0],["chair",0],["hop",1],["drink",1],["laugh",1],["sit",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagnv","caption":"Flagship lab: find nouns in a park, sort person, place and thing, make Sam do verbs, test words and build sentences.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_VY3rBq8RyP":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"The dog sleeps below the table. Which words tell where?","choices":["under the table","on the table","in the table"],"answer":0,"hint":"Below means the same as which word?","explain":"Under the table. Under means below."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"What is the opposite of on?","choices":["off","in","up"],"answer":0,"hint":"You put a hat on. Then you take it ...","explain":"Off is the opposite of on. You put a hat on, then take it off."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Mia stands at the side of her friend. Where is she?","choices":["next to her friend","under her friend","in her friend"],"answer":0,"hint":"At the side means the same as which words?","explain":"Next to her friend. Next to means at the side."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"The cat jumps down from the wall. Which words tell which way?","choices":["off the wall","on the wall","in the wall"],"answer":0,"hint":"Moving down and away from the top is which word?","explain":"Off the wall. Off means moving down and away from the top of something."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"{n} children walk ___ school in the morning. Which word fits?","choices":["to","from","off"],"answer":0,"hint":"In the morning they are going towards school.","explain":"The {n} children walk to school. To means going towards it.","params":{"n":[3,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Put the ball in the box. Where does it go?","choices":["Inside the box","On top of the box","Beside the box"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what the little word in means.","explain":"Inside the box. In means inside. On top would be on, and beside would be next to."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"{n} fish swim ___ the net and get away. Which word fits?","choices":["out of","into","on"],"answer":0,"hint":"They get away, so are they going inside or leaving?","explain":"The {n} fish swim out of the net. Out of means leaving the inside.","params":{"n":[2,7]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"The book sits on top of the table. Which words tell where?","choices":["on the table","in the table","under the table"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is the book on top, inside, or below?","explain":"On the table. On means on top. In is inside, and under is below."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"There are {n} socks inside the drawer. Where are the socks?","choices":["in the drawer","on the drawer","next to the drawer"],"answer":0,"hint":"Inside means the same as which little word?","explain":"The {n} socks are in the drawer. In means inside.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"What is the opposite of in?","choices":["out","on","to"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of going in a door, then the other way.","explain":"Out is the opposite of in. You go in the door, then you come out."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"{n} ducks walk ___ the pond and splash. Which word fits?","choices":["into","out of","under"],"answer":0,"hint":"Splash means they go in the water.","explain":"The {n} ducks walk into the pond. Into means going inside it.","params":{"n":[2,5]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Some little words tell where things are. The cat is on the mat. The ball is under the bed. The fish is in the bowl.","Next to means at the side. The cup is next to the plate. They sit side by side.","Some little words tell which way to go. The frog hops off the log. It jumps into the pond. Then it hops to the flower.","Some of these words are opposites. In and out. On and off. Up and down. To and from."],"adult":["A preposition links a noun to the rest of a sentence, usually to show place, direction or time. US Common Core asks kindergarteners to use the most frequent prepositions (to, from, in, out, on, off, for, of, by, with) and first graders to use frequently occurring prepositions such as during, beyond and toward. In England, children meet position and direction language in Year 1 maths and use prepositions in writing from Year 3.","A preposition plus its noun group is a prepositional phrase: 'under the bed', 'into the pond'. Young children understand these from action first, so moving real objects to follow a spoken phrase is the best practice. Contrast pairs (in versus on, to versus from) build precision faster than single words.","Common mistakes: mixing up in and on (in means inside, on means on top of a surface); using next to and behind as if they meant the same; thinking 'off' only means a switch, not moving down from something; and mixing up to and from, which point opposite ways. Say the phrase and act it out to check.","In the lab your child drags a cat on, under, in and next to; names where a ball is; matches opposite pairs; hops a frog off a log, into a pond, out of it and to a flower; and follows tidy-up instructions."]},"familyTip":"Play 'Teddy says': ask your child to put a teddy on the chair, under the table, in a box and next to the door. Then swap roles so your child gives you the instructions.","keyIdea":"Prepositions are little words that tell where something is or which way it goes. Position words such as on, under, in and next to say where a thing is. Direction words such as to, from, into, out of and off say which way it moves. Many come in opposite pairs: in and out, on and off, up and down, to and from.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does each word tell where it is, or which way it goes? Drag each word, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["📍 Where it is","➡️ Which way it goes"],"items":[["on the mat",0],["under the bed",0],["in the box",0],["next to the cup",0],["into the pond",1],["off the log",1],["to the park",1],["out of the car",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagprep","caption":"Flagship lab: move the cat, say where the ball is, match opposites, hop the frog and tidy the room.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_18qkgxr_-T":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"One wish. Two ...?","choices":["wishes","wishs","wish"],"answer":0,"hint":"Wish ends in sh. Clap it: how many beats?","explain":"Wishes. Wish ends in sh, so it takes -es: wish-es, two beats."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"One hen. {n} ...?","choices":["hens","henes","hen"],"answer":0,"hint":"Hen ends in n. Does it hiss or buzz?","explain":"{n} hens. Hen just takes -s. Hens has one beat.","params":{"n":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"There is 1 ... on the plate. Which word fits?","choices":["apple","apples","applees"],"answer":0,"hint":"How many are there? One thing needs no ending.","explain":"1 apple. There is only one, so the word has no ending."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"One bus. {n} ...?","choices":["buses","buss","bus"],"answer":0,"hint":"Bus already ends in s. What do s words take?","explain":"{n} buses. Bus ends in s, so we add -es. Bus-es has two beats.","params":{"n":[2,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which word has an extra beat when you say it?","choices":["foxes","frogs","cats"],"answer":0,"hint":"Clap each one. Which one needs two claps?","explain":"Foxes: fox-es, two claps. Frogs and cats are one clap, so they only take -s."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Plural means ...","choices":["more than one","just one","a word that ends in s"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of one cat and two cats. Which one is plural?","explain":"Plural means more than one. A word can end in s and still be just one thing, like bus."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"In 'Two kids ride the bus', which word is a plural noun?","choices":["kids","bus","ride"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the thing there is more than one of.","explain":"Kids is the plural: there are two of them. Bus ends in s but is one bus, and ride is a doing word."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"One dog. Two ...?","choices":["dogs","doges","dog"],"answer":0,"hint":"Dog ends in g. Does it hiss or buzz at the end?","explain":"Dogs. Dog does not end in a hiss or buzz sound, so it just takes -s."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"One box. {n} ...?","choices":["boxes","boxs","box"],"answer":0,"hint":"Box ends in x. Say the plural and clap the beats.","explain":"{n} boxes. Box ends in x, so we add -es. Box-es has two beats.","params":{"n":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which word means more than one?","choices":["cups","bus","glass"],"answer":0,"hint":"Two of the words end in s but name just one thing.","explain":"Cups means more than one cup. Bus and glass end in s, but each is one thing."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Sam has {n} brush... Which ending?","choices":["-es: brushes","-s: brushs","no ending: brush"],"answer":0,"hint":"Brush ends in sh. What do sh words take?","explain":"{n} brushes. Brush ends in sh, so it takes -es, and you hear an extra beat.","params":{"n":[2,8]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A noun is a word for a thing. Cat is a noun. Box is a noun.","One cat. Two cats. When there is more than one, we add s to the end. More than one is called plural.","Some words end in a hiss or a buzz sound, like fox, bus, wish and watch. They get es: foxes, buses, wishes, watches. Say fox-es and clap. You hear one more beat.","Bus ends in s, but it is just one bus. One thing gets no s. One apple. Three apples."],"adult":["Plural nouns name more than one thing. In England, Year 1 pupils learn the regular plural noun suffixes -s and -es (dog, dogs; wish, wishes), and the spelling rule is taught by sound: if the ending is heard as /s/ or /z/ it is spelt -s; if it is /ɪz/ and adds an extra syllable, it is spelt -es. US Common Core asks kindergarteners to form regular plurals orally by adding /s/ or /es/ (L.K.1c).","The -es words are the ones whose singular ends in a sibilant sound: s, ss, x, z, sh or ch (bus, kiss, box, buzz, dish, bench). Clapping syllables is the easiest check, because the -es always brings its own beat. Irregular plurals (mouse, mice; child, children) and the y to ies change come later, so leave them out for now.","Common mistakes: writing foxs or wishs because the child adds -s to everything; adding -es to words that do not hiss or buzz (penes); adding an ending when there is only one (1 apples); and thinking any word that ends in s is plural, such as bus or grass. Verbs like takes also end in s but are doing words, not plural nouns.","In the lab your child adds animals to a pen and sees the s appear, drags -s or -es onto six words, sorts plurals by one clap or two, finds the real plurals in sentences, and finishes a shop list where one thing gets no ending."]},"familyTip":"Play 'one, two' at tea time: you say 'one cup', your child says 'two cups'. Mix in hissing words like dish, glass and box, and clap together to hear the extra beat in dish-es.","keyIdea":"A noun names a thing. Singular means one; plural means more than one. Most nouns add -s to make the plural (cat, cats). Nouns that end in s, x, z, sh or ch add -es (fox, foxes; wish, wishes), and you can hear the extra beat. One thing gets no ending, and a word that ends in s, like bus, is not always plural.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does each word take -s or -es to mean more than one? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Add -s","Add -es"],"items":[["cat",0],["dog",0],["hat",0],["bell",0],["fox",1],["dish",1],["bus",1],["watch",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagplural","caption":"Flagship lab: fill the pens, drag -s or -es, clap the beats, find the plurals and finish the shop list.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_uorNrPTh6U":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which word gives a reason?","choices":["because","while","after"],"answer":0,"hint":"One word answers the question 'why?'.","explain":"Because gives a reason. While and after are time conjunctions."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"'Ben was tired, ___ he went to bed early.' Which word fits?","choices":["so","because","before"],"answer":0,"hint":"Being tired came first. Going to bed is what happened as a result.","explain":"So. 'So' comes in front of the result. 'Because' would mean going to bed made Ben tired."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which is the best start for the last sentence of a story?","choices":["Finally,","First,","Next,"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which time adverb means the very last thing?","explain":"Finally means the last thing. First opens a story, and next moves it on."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which sentence has the comma in the right place?","choices":["After the film ended, we went home.","After the film, ended we went home.","After, the film ended we went home."],"answer":0,"hint":"The comma goes at the end of the fronted clause.","explain":"'After the film ended, we went home.' The comma marks where the fronted clause stops."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Sam read {n} pages while he waited. What does 'while' tell us?","choices":["He read and waited at the same time","He read before he waited","He read because he waited"],"answer":0,"hint":"While is a time word. Does it mean before, after or at the same time?","explain":"While means at the same time. Sam read {n} pages during the wait.","params":{"n":[3,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"The match was stopped after {n} minutes because of the snow. What does 'because of the snow' tell us?","choices":["Why it was stopped","When it was stopped","Where it was stopped"],"answer":0,"hint":"Because of starts a phrase that gives a reason.","explain":"Why. 'Because of the snow' is the cause. 'After {n} minutes' is the phrase that tells when.","params":{"n":[10,40]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"'Ali packed his bag ___ he left for school.' Which word fits?","choices":["before","after","so"],"answer":0,"hint":"He needs his bag at school. Which happened first?","explain":"Before. Packing came first. 'After' would mean he packed once he had already gone."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which word fits? 'The pond froze ___ it was so cold.'","choices":["because","so","before"],"answer":0,"hint":"The cold is the reason. Which word comes before a reason?","explain":"Because. The cold is the cause, and 'because' comes in front of the cause."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Mia waited {n} minutes. The bus was late. Which sentence joins these the right way?","choices":["Mia waited {n} minutes because the bus was late.","Mia waited {n} minutes so the bus was late.","The bus was late because Mia waited {n} minutes."],"answer":0,"hint":"What is the reason Mia waited? Put 'because' in front of the reason.","explain":"The late bus is the cause, so it goes after 'because': Mia waited {n} minutes because the bus was late.","params":{"n":[5,20]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which phrase tells WHEN?","choices":["during the match","under the table","because of the wind"],"answer":0,"hint":"Time phrases often start with during, before, after or on.","explain":"During the match tells when. Under the table tells where, and because of the wind tells why."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"The cake baked for {n} minutes. Then we ate it. Which word fits? '___ the cake had baked for {n} minutes, we ate it.'","choices":["After","Before","Because"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which came first, the baking or the eating?","explain":"After. The baking came first, so 'After the cake had baked for {n} minutes, we ate it.'","params":{"n":[20,40]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Some words tell us when. Before, after, while and when are time words. 'Wash your hands before you eat.'","Some words tell us why. Because gives the reason. So gives what happened next. 'It was cold, so I wore a hat.' 'I wore a hat because it was cold.'","Take care. Swap because and so, and the sentence goes back to front. 'It was cold because I wore a hat' is silly!","Do not start each sentence with then. Use first, next, after that and finally. They help the reader follow the order. Short phrases can say when, where or why too: during the film, under the bed, because of the rain."],"adult":["In the English national curriculum, Year 3 pupils learn to express time, place and cause using conjunctions (when, before, after, while, so, because), adverbs (then, next, soon, therefore) and prepositions (before, after, during, in, because of). The grammar terms they meet are conjunction, clause, subordinate clause, adverb and preposition.","A conjunction such as because or after joins a main clause to a subordinate clause: 'We went inside because it was raining.' A time adverb links ideas across sentences: 'First, she opened the door. Then, she stepped outside.' A preposition starts a phrase: 'during the lesson', 'behind the shed', 'because of the noise'.","The commonest slip is swapping because and so. 'Because' comes before the cause; 'so' comes before the result, so swapping them reverses the meaning. Another is a 'then, then, then' story where every sentence starts the same way. A third is muddling before and after, which flips the order of events.","When a subordinate clause moves to the front ('After the bell rang, we lined up'), a comma marks where it ends. Pupils often put it in the wrong place. The lab practises each idea: joining clauses, ordering a story with time adverbs, sorting phrases into when, where and why, building cause-and-result sentences, and placing the comma."]},"familyTip":"At bedtime, retell the day using first, next, after that and finally. Then play 'because or so': say half a sentence like 'I was hungry…' and take turns finishing it with 'so…' and then with 'because…'.","keyIdea":"Small joining words tell the reader when, where and why. Conjunctions like when, before, after and while show time; because and so show cause. Adverbs like first, next, soon and therefore link one sentence to the next. Prepositions start phrases like during the lesson (when), under the bridge (where) and because of the rain (why).","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does the bold word show time or cause? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["⏰ Time (when?)","❓ Cause (why?)"],"items":[["We lined up after the bell rang.",0],["Wash your hands before you eat.",0],["I hummed while I painted.",0],["Soon, the sun came out.",0],["I wore a coat because it was cold.",1],["It rained, so the match stopped.",1],["We stayed in because of the storm.",1],["He forgot his key. Therefore, he knocked.",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagwhen","caption":"Flagship lab: join clauses with conjunctions, fix a 'then, then' story, sort when, where and why phrases, and place a fronted clause's comma.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_N8CpN1EJrP":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What does a sentence tell?","choices":["A whole idea","One word","Just a name"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about 'The dog runs.' It makes sense on its own.","explain":"A sentence tells a whole idea: who, and what they do. One word or a name is only a piece."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which one is NOT a whole sentence?","choices":["The big blue ball","The ball rolls.","Kim kicks the ball."],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for the one with no doing word.","explain":"'The big blue ball' names a thing, but it does not say what happens."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Make 'The frog' a whole sentence.","choices":["The frog hops.","The green frog","The frog the pond"],"answer":0,"hint":"Add a doing word that says what the frog does.","explain":"'hops' is a doing word, so 'The frog hops.' is whole. 'green' and 'the pond' do not say what it does."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"'The {n} birds sing in the tree.' How many sentences is this?","choices":["One","Two","None"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the who and the doing words.","explain":"One. 'in the tree' is a detail about where. There is one who (the {n} birds) and one doing word (sing), so one sentence.","params":{"n":[2,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which one is written the right way?","choices":["The sun is hot.","the sun is hot.","The sun is hot"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the start and the end.","explain":"It starts with a capital letter (The) and ends with a full stop."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Mia makes {n} sentences. Each has a who. What else does each need?","choices":["A doing word","Another who","A picture"],"answer":0,"hint":"A sentence has a who, and then what they do.","explain":"Each of the {n} sentences needs a doing word, to say what the who does.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Add a detail to 'The dog runs.' Which is best?","choices":["The dog runs in the park.","The dog runs. In the park.","In the park."],"answer":0,"hint":"Keep the detail inside the one sentence.","explain":"'The dog runs in the park.' keeps one sentence. 'In the park.' on its own is not finished."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which one is a whole sentence?","choices":["The cat naps.","The fat cat","naps on a rug"],"answer":0,"hint":"A whole sentence says who, and what they do.","explain":"'The cat naps.' says who (the cat) and what it does (naps). The others each miss a piece."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"There are {n} ducks. 'The {n} ducks' is not finished. What is missing?","choices":["What the ducks do","Who it is about","Nothing is missing"],"answer":0,"hint":"We know who. Do we know what they do?","explain":"We know who: the {n} ducks. We do not know what they do, so add a doing word: The {n} ducks swim.","params":{"n":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"'jumps in the mud' is not finished. What is missing?","choices":["Who jumps","A doing word","Nothing is missing"],"answer":0,"hint":"Jumps is the doing word. Who is doing it?","explain":"Jumps is the doing word, but we do not know who. Add a who: The pig jumps in the mud."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Sam has {n} sentences to write. Where does the full stop go in each one?","choices":["At the end","After the first word","At the start"],"answer":0,"hint":"The full stop tells you to stop.","explain":"A full stop goes at the end of each of the {n} sentences, after the last word.","params":{"n":[2,5]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Words join up to make a sentence. A sentence tells a whole idea. 'The dog runs.' is a sentence.","A sentence needs two parts. Who is it about? The dog. What does it do? Runs. Put them together: The dog runs.","'The dog' is not finished. You want to ask, what does the dog do? 'jumps high' is not finished too. Who jumps?","You can make a sentence bigger. The cat sleeps on the mat. The cat sleeps all day. It is still one sentence. When you write it, start with a big capital letter. Stop with a full stop at the end."],"adult":["In England, Year 1 pupils learn how words combine to make sentences, using the terms word, sentence, capital letter, full stop and punctuation; US Common Core asks kindergarteners to produce and expand complete sentences in shared language activities (L.K.1f). The grammar underneath is a subject (who or what) plus a verb (what they do), which together express a complete thought.","The quickest test is to say it aloud and listen: if you are left asking 'who?' or 'what happened?', it is a fragment, not a sentence. 'The big red bus' names a thing but has no verb; 'ran to the park' has a verb but no subject. Children can often hear this before they can explain it, so talk comes first and writing second.","Common mistakes: treating any group of words as a sentence because it starts with a capital letter; thinking a longer sentence must be two sentences, and putting a full stop before an added detail ('The cat sleeps. On the mat.'); full stops after every word or line; and a lower-case first letter. Expanding with a where, when or how phrase keeps one subject and one verb, so it stays one sentence.","In the lab your child couples a who car and a doing car to make word trains, sorts whole sentences from unfinished bits, mends three fragments with the missing piece, stretches 'The cat sleeps.' with details, and adds the capital letter and the full stop."]},"familyTip":"Play 'finish my sentence': say 'The cat…' and stop. Your child adds what the cat does. Swap: they start with a doing word ('…jumps!') and you add who. Then make it bigger together: where? when?","keyIdea":"A sentence is a group of words that tells a complete thought. It says who or what (the dog) and what they do (runs). A bit with a piece missing, like 'The dog' or 'jumps high', is not a whole sentence yet. You can add details about where, when or how, and it is still one sentence. A written sentence starts with a capital letter and ends with a full stop.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is it a whole sentence, or not finished yet? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["✅ Whole sentence","🧩 Not finished"],"items":[["The dog barks.",0],["Sam reads.",0],["The ducks swim.",0],["Mum laughs.",0],["The little duck",1],["eats a cake",1],["in the box",1],["My red hat",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagsent","caption":"Flagship lab: couple who and doing-word cars into sentences, sort whole from unfinished, fix fragments, add details and put in the capital letter and full stop.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_0QJoKWABdC":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which sentence is punctuated correctly?","choices":["Before breakfast, she packed her bag.","Before, breakfast she packed her bag.","Before breakfast she, packed her bag."],"answer":0,"hint":"The comma goes after the whole opener.","explain":"The opener is \"Before breakfast\", so the comma goes after breakfast. The other two split the opener or the subject and verb."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which part is the adverbial? \"A troll was waiting under the bridge.\"","choices":["under the bridge","A troll","was waiting"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which part tells where the troll was?","explain":"\"Under the bridge\" tells where the troll waited, so it is the adverbial. \"A troll\" is the subject and \"was waiting\" is the verb."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which sentence needs no comma?","choices":["The fox ran home at midnight.","At midnight the fox ran home.","Quietly the fox ran home."],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for the sentence where the adverbial is at the end.","explain":"In \"The fox ran home at midnight\" the adverbial is at the end, so no comma is needed. The other two have fronted adverbials and need one."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"What is wrong with \"The children, ran home after school.\"?","choices":["The comma splits the subject from its verb","It needs a comma after school","Nothing is wrong"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is there a fronted adverbial at the start?","explain":"There is no fronted adverbial, and a comma must not come between the children and ran."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"A fronted adverbial has {n} words. How many commas go after it?","answer":"1","hint":"Short or long, the opener gets the same punctuation.","explain":"Whether it has {n} words or one, a fronted adverbial gets exactly 1 comma, straight after its last word.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which opener tells where?","choices":["In the dark cave,","Next week,","As fast as he could,"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one names a place you could point to?","explain":"\"In the dark cave\" names a place, so it tells where. \"Next week\" tells when and \"As fast as he could\" tells how."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Sam's story has {n} sentences that all start with \"The dog\". He fronts an adverbial in {k} of them. How many still start with \"The dog\"?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Each fronted adverbial gives one sentence a new start.","explain":"{n} take away {k} new openings leaves {=n-k} sentences still starting with \"The dog\".","params":{"n":[4,7],"k":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Move the adverbial to the front: \"We went swimming after school.\"","choices":["After school, we went swimming.","after school, we went swimming.","After school we, went swimming."],"answer":0,"hint":"The opener gets the capital letter and a comma after it.","explain":"\"After school\" moves to the front with a capital A and a comma after it; \"we\" becomes lower case."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Where does the comma go after the fronted adverbial in \"Later that day the children ran home\"?","choices":["After day","After Later","After children"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the whole opener that tells when. Where does it end?","explain":"The fronted adverbial is \"Later that day\", so the comma goes after day: Later that day, the children ran home."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A paragraph has {n} sentences. {k} of them start with a fronted adverbial. How many commas after fronted adverbials should it have?","answer":"k","hint":"Each fronted adverbial gets one comma after it. The other sentences do not need one for this rule.","explain":"Each of the {k} fronted adverbials needs one comma after it, so the paragraph needs {=k} of these commas, whatever the other {=n-k} sentences do.","params":{"n":[5,8],"k":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"\"Silently, the cat crept closer.\" What does the adverbial \"Silently\" tell us?","choices":["How the cat moved","When the cat moved","Where the cat moved"],"answer":0,"hint":"Ask: in what way did the cat creep?","explain":"Silently tells how the cat crept. A when adverbial would be like \"At dawn\", and a where one like \"Under the table\"."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Jo writes {n} sentences. She fronts the adverbial in {k} of them. How many still start with the subject?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Take the sentences with a fronted adverbial away from the total.","explain":"{n} sentences take away {k} with a fronted adverbial leaves {=n-k} that start with the subject.","params":{"n":[5,8],"k":[1,4]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Some parts of a sentence tell when, where or how. At midnight tells when. Under the bridge tells where. Silently tells how. These parts are called adverbials. An adverbial can be one word or a group of words.","You can move an adverbial to the front: The fox crept through the garden at midnight. At midnight, the fox crept through the garden. Now it is a fronted adverbial. It starts the sentence, so it gets the capital letter.","Put a comma straight after the whole opener. Later that day, the children ran home. Not Later, that day. The comma shows the reader where the opener stops and the main part starts.","Fronted adverbials help you start sentences in new ways. Mix them up: After tea, Under the stairs, As fast as he could. If the adverbial stays at the end, you do not need a comma."],"adult":["In the English National Curriculum, fronted adverbials and the comma after them are Year 4 grammar and punctuation, listed in English Appendix 2 with the example 'Later that day, I heard the bad news.' Pupils learn the term adverbial and use fronted adverbials to vary sentence openings. US Common Core L.5.2b, using a comma to separate an introductory element from the rest of the sentence, covers the same rule.","An adverbial can be a single adverb (silently), a prepositional phrase (under the bridge, after lunch) or a subordinate clause (as fast as he could). Children often think only -ly words count. Ask what the chunk tells: when, where or how. If it answers one of those and can move, it is an adverbial.","Common mistakes: putting the comma inside the phrase ('Later, that day the children…'), leaving it out, and putting a comma between the subject and the verb ('The children, ran home'). Children also forget that the fronted word takes the capital letter. Be careful too with a page where every sentence now starts with a fronted adverbial; variety means mixing them with other openings.","In the lab your child taps the adverbial that tells when, where or how in three sentences, then drags an adverbial to the front and sees the capital letter and comma appear. They tap the correct comma gap in four sentences and judge six sentences as correct or needing a fix. Last, they sort six openers into when, where and how."]},"familyTip":"Take a plain sentence from a reading book, like \"We walked to the shop.\" Take turns adding a fronted adverbial and saying it with a pause where the comma goes: \"After breakfast, we walked to the shop.\" \"In the pouring rain, we walked to the shop.\"","keyIdea":"An adverbial is a word or phrase that tells when, where or how something happens: at midnight, under the bridge, silently. Moved to the start of a sentence, it becomes a fronted adverbial, and a comma goes straight after the whole of it: \"Later that day, the children ran home.\" The opener takes the capital letter. An adverbial left at the end needs no comma, and a comma never goes between the subject and its verb.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is the comma right? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["✓ Correct","✗ Needs fixing"],"items":[["At the weekend, we visited Gran.",0],["Carefully, she opened the box.",0],["As the sun set, the owls woke up.",0],["We visited Gran at the weekend.",0],["At the weekend we visited Gran.",1],["Carefully she, opened the box.",1],["As the sun, set the owls woke up.",1],["The owls, woke up as the sun set.",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagfront","caption":"Flagship lab: find the adverbial, drag it to the front, place the comma, judge sentences and sort openers into when, where and how.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_QEr24lqzvH":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What goes at the start of every sentence?","choices":["A capital letter","A full stop","A question mark"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the big T in The cat sat on the mat.","explain":"Every sentence starts with a capital letter. The marks go at the end."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which mark ends this sentence? Can you see the bird","choices":["?","!","."],"answer":0,"hint":"Does it tell, ask or shout?","explain":"Can you see the bird asks something, so it ends with a question mark ?"},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Where does the full stop go? I like red apples","choices":["After apples","After like","After red"],"answer":0,"hint":"The full stop goes where the idea is finished.","explain":"After apples, the last word. I like. is not a finished idea, so a full stop after like is too soon."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which line has two sentences written the right way?","choices":["It was hot. We had ice cream.","It was hot we had ice cream.","It was hot. we had ice cream."],"answer":0,"hint":"Each idea needs a full stop and the next one a capital letter.","explain":"It was hot. We had ice cream. has a full stop after hot and a capital W for the new sentence."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which mark ends this sentence? Help, the boat is sinking","choices":["!","?","."],"answer":0,"hint":"How would you say it if it were true?","explain":"Help, the boat is sinking is a shout with a big feeling, so it ends with an exclamation mark !"},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A story has {a} telling sentences, {b} questions and 1 exclamation. How many full stops does it have?","answer":"a","hint":"Questions get ? and the exclamation gets !. Only telling sentences end with a full stop.","explain":"Only the {a} telling sentences end with a full stop, so there are {a} full stops.","params":{"a":[2,5],"b":[1,3]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Sam wrote {n} sentences. He forgot the capital letter on 1 of them. How many sentences start the right way?","answer":"n-1","hint":"Take away the one with no capital letter.","explain":"{n} take away 1 is {=n-1}, so {=n-1} sentences start with a capital letter.","params":{"n":[3,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which word needs a capital letter? the dog ate my hat.","choices":["the","dog","hat"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which word comes first? The first word gets the capital.","explain":"the is the first word, so it needs a capital: The dog ate my hat."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which sentence is written the right way?","choices":["The dog ran fast.","the dog ran fast.","The dog ran fast"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the first letter and the very end.","explain":"The dog ran fast. starts with a capital T and ends with a full stop, so it is right."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Ali writes {a} telling sentences and {b} asking sentences. How many question marks does he need?","answer":"b","hint":"Only the asking sentences end with a question mark.","explain":"Only the {b} asking sentences need a question mark. The {a} telling ones end with full stops. So he needs {b}.","params":{"a":[1,4],"b":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which mark ends this sentence? What a huge cake that is","choices":["!","?","."],"answer":0,"hint":"Is it asking something, or showing surprise?","explain":"It starts with What, but it does not ask. It shows surprise, so it ends with an exclamation mark !"},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Mia writes {n} sentences. How many capital letters does she need at the start of her sentences?","answer":"n","hint":"Every sentence starts with one capital letter.","explain":"Each sentence starts with one capital letter, so {n} sentences need {n} capitals at the start.","params":{"n":[2,6]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A sentence starts with a big letter. It is called a capital letter. The cat sat on the mat. See the big T?","A sentence ends with a mark. It shows the idea is done. A dot is called a full stop. Put it after the last word, not in the middle.","Some sentences ask. Can you see the moon? Asking ends with a question mark. Telling ends with a full stop. Some shout or feel big. What a big cake this is! Ouch, that hurts! These end with an exclamation mark.","Two ideas need two sentences. We went to the park. We fed the ducks. Stop after park. Then start with a capital."],"adult":["This is sentence demarcation. In England's National Curriculum, Year 1 pupils begin to punctuate sentences using a capital letter and a full stop, question mark or exclamation mark, and use a capital letter for names and the pronoun I. Year 2 adds the four sentence forms: statement, question, exclamation and command. US Common Core L.K.2 and L.1.2 ask for the same capitals and end punctuation.","Teach the marks by what the sentence does, not by its length. A statement tells, a question asks and needs an answer, and an exclamation shows strong feeling or surprise. In the grammar tests used in England, an exclamation sentence starts with What or How and has a verb, as in What a big cake this is! Short cries such as Ouch! and commands such as Stop! may also take an exclamation mark.","Common mistakes: no capital at the start, a capital in the middle of a sentence, a full stop at the end of a line rather than at the end of the idea, a full stop on a question, and two ideas run together with no full stop. Another slip is reading What a big cake this is as a question because it starts with What.","In the lab your child taps the first word to give it a capital, drags a full stop to the end of the idea, picks a full stop, question mark or exclamation mark by how each sentence sounds, and splits squashed lines into two sentences."]},"familyTip":"When you read together, stop at each full stop and take a breath, and make your voice go up at a question mark. Then ask your child: how should this one end?","keyIdea":"Every sentence starts with a capital letter and ends with a mark. A telling sentence (a statement) ends with a full stop. An asking sentence (a question) ends with a question mark. A sentence that shows surprise, a big feeling or a shout ends with an exclamation mark. When two ideas are squashed together, put a full stop after the first one and a capital letter at the start of the next.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"How does each sentence end? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["🙂 Telling: full stop .","🤔 Asking: question mark ?","😮 Big feeling: exclamation mark !"],"items":[["The sun is hot",0],["I have a red coat",0],["Can you swim",1],["Where is Dad",1],["Is it time for tea",1],["What a big dog you have",2],["Ouch, my toe",2],["We went to the park",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagstop","caption":"Flagship lab: give sentences a capital letter, drag full stops, pick . ? or ! by how a sentence sounds, and split squashed lines into two sentences.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_YXVQaufkKO":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What job does 'and' do?","choices":["It joins words and ideas","It ends a sentence","It asks a question"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of 'cats and dogs'. What does 'and' do there?","explain":"'And' is a joining word, a conjunction. It joins two things or two ideas."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which list is written the right way?","choices":["I ate eggs, toast and jam.","I ate eggs and toast and and jam.","I ate eggs, toast, jam and."],"answer":0,"hint":"Commas between things. 'And' just before the last one.","explain":"Eggs, toast and jam: a comma between the first two, and 'and' before the last thing."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Join 'I got up.' and 'I had my breakfast.'","choices":["I got up and I had my breakfast.","I got up and i had my breakfast.","I got up. And I had my breakfast and."],"answer":0,"hint":"Does 'I' ever get a small letter?","explain":"One full stop at the end, and 'I' always keeps its capital letter, even in the middle."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which ideas go together?","choices":["I was hungry and I ate a sandwich.","I was hungry and the sky is blue.","I was hungry and the car is fast."],"answer":0,"hint":"Does the second idea have something to do with the first?","explain":"Being hungry and eating a sandwich go together. The other endings have nothing to do with being hungry."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which is easiest to read?","choices":["We went to the zoo. We saw a lion and it roared.","We went to the zoo and we saw a lion and it roared and we went home and we slept.","We went to the zoo we saw a lion it roared."],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for short sentences with a full stop at the end of each.","explain":"Too many ands make one long chain. Short sentences, with one 'and' at most, are easy to follow."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A sentence has {n} ands in a long chain. You change all but one of them into full stops. How many ands are left?","answer":"1","hint":"All but one means you keep just one.","explain":"You change {=n-1} of the {n} ands into full stops, so 1 'and' is left.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"You write {n} short sentences. Then you join two of them with 'and'. How many sentences are there now?","answer":"n-1","hint":"Two sentences become one, so you have one fewer.","explain":"Joining two sentences turns 2 into 1, so {n} sentences become {=n-1}.","params":{"n":[3,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Where does 'and' go in a list of four things?","choices":["Just before the last thing","Between every thing","Right at the start"],"answer":0,"hint":"The other gaps get commas.","explain":"Commas go between the first things, and 'and' goes just before the last one: red, blue, green and pink."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Where does 'and' go? I like ___ apples ___ pears ___.","choices":["I like apples and pears.","I like and apples pears.","I like apples pears and."],"answer":0,"hint":"'And' joins two things. Put it between them.","explain":"'And' sits between the two things it joins: apples and pears."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Zara joins {n} pairs of short sentences with 'and'. Each pair becomes one sentence. How many full stops are there now?","answer":"n","hint":"Each joined sentence has one full stop, at the end.","explain":"Each pair becomes one sentence with one full stop, so {n} pairs make {n} sentences and {n} full stops.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Join 'The sun came out.' and 'We went to the park.'","choices":["The sun came out and we went to the park.","The sun came out. and we went to the park.","The sun came and out we went to the park."],"answer":0,"hint":"Take out the first full stop. Put 'and' where it was.","explain":"The first full stop goes, 'and' joins the two ideas, and 'We' becomes 'we' because it is not the start any more."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A list has {n} things in it. How many commas does it need, with 'and' before the last thing?","answer":"n-2","hint":"Count the gaps between things. The last gap gets 'and', not a comma.","explain":"{n} things have {=n-1} gaps. The last gap gets 'and', so {=n-2} gaps get a comma.","params":{"n":[3,7]}}],"explain":{"kid":["'And' is a joining word. It joins two things. I like cats and dogs. 'And' goes in the middle, between the two things.","'And' can join two sentences. The dog barked. The cat ran. Now make one: The dog barked and the cat ran. One sentence has one full stop, at the end.","In a list, put a comma between the things. Put 'and' just before the last one. I packed a hat, a coat and a scarf.","Join ideas that go together. I was cold and I put on my coat. Do not use too many ands. If it gets long, stop and start a new sentence."],"adult":["The English national curriculum (Appendix 2) asks Year 1 pupils to join words and join clauses using 'and'. Their terms are word, sentence, full stop and capital letter. Year 2 adds co-ordination with 'or', 'and' and 'but', and commas to separate items in a list, with the terms statement and comma. The word conjunction is a pupil term from Year 3. Before that, 'joining word' is the usual name.","'And' is a co-ordinating conjunction. It links two words, two phrases or two main clauses of equal weight. When two sentences become one, the first full stop goes. The next word loses its capital letter, unless it is 'I' or a name. In a list of three or more things, school style puts commas between the items and 'and' before the last one. A comma before that last 'and' (the Oxford comma) is optional.","Common mistakes: putting 'and' before both items or at the end ('and cats dogs'); leaving the first full stop in ('I ran. and I jumped'); writing a small 'i' in the middle; using 'and' between every item in a list; joining ideas with no link; and the very common chain of 'and then… and then… and then', where one sentence runs on and on.","In the lab your child drags 'and' between two things and joins two sentences, then sees the full stop go. They build lists with a comma and an 'and', and pick the idea that fits after 'and'. Last, they tap extra ands to turn a long chain into short sentences."]},"familyTip":"Tell each other about your day in two short sentences, then join them with 'and'. When a story turns into 'and then… and then…', clap where a new sentence should start.","keyIdea":"'And' is a joining word (a conjunction). It goes between two things to join them: cats and dogs. It can join two sentences into one longer sentence: 'The dog barked and the cat ran.' The full stop in the middle goes, and the second part starts with a small letter, except 'I' and names. In a list of three or more things, commas go between the things and 'and' goes only before the last one: a hat, a coat and a scarf. 'And' joins ideas that go together, and a long chain of ands is hard to follow, so start a new sentence instead.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is 'and' used the right way? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["✅ Joined the right way","🔧 Needs fixing"],"items":[["I like cats and dogs.",0],["I like and cats dogs.",1],["We swam and we had chips.",0],["We swam. and we had chips.",1],["I see a cat, a dog and a hen.",0],["I see a cat and, a dog a hen.",1],["It rained and we got wet.",0],["It rained and my pen is red.",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagand","caption":"Flagship lab: put 'and' between two things, join two sentences, build lists, pick ideas that go together and break up a chain of ands.","opts":{"step":0}}}});
